  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThrough Tonight\n\nBroken cumulus and stratocumulus clouds are expected through \nthis afternoon as some 850 mb moisture works it way south on the\nbackside of a departing 850 mb trough. With the loss of diurnal\nheating this evening, skies will quickly clear.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the lower and mid-70s. It will be\njust a bit warmer tonight than this morning with low\ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s. The \ncoldest temperatures will be central and north-central\nWisconsin.\n \nThursday through Friday \n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather through Friday. Even \nthough there is northwest flow aloft, there appears to be very \nlittle smoke will be impacting the area though at least \nThursday. Toward the end of the latest HRRR run, it does appear \nthat elevated smoke concentrations are increasing across \nAlberta, British Columbia, and Montana. This might be a hint \nthat we could start to see the elevated smoke concentrations \nincreasing on Friday in the northwest flow aloft. However, with \nsoutherly flow near the surface, still not seeing any hints that\nthis smoke will become surface-based.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-70s to lower 80s and \nlow temperatures will mainly be in the 50s and lower 60s.\n\nFriday Night into Saturday\n\nThe models are continuing to show that a northern stream\nshortwave trough will move east along the US/Canadian border.\nAs this occurs, a cold front will drop southward toward\nInterstate 94. This may result in some isolated to scattered\nshowers and storms for areas along and north of Interstate 94.\nThere are even some solutions hinting that they may get as far\nsouth of Interstate 90. Low temperatures will be in the lower\nand mid-60s.\n\nWith rising 500 mb heights, this front should head back to the\nnorth on Saturday. High temperatures will be mainly in the 80s\nwith heat indices ranging from the mid-80s to upper 90s. There \nmight be even a few lower 90s.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday\n\nThe models continue to show that a mesoscale convective complex\nwill likely develop over the Northern Plains and then move\nsoutheast through the Upper Mississippi Valley on Saturday \nnight and Sunday morning. Still plenty of uncertainty on the \ntrack of this system. As far as severe weather, the 3-6 km shear\nremains generally weak in the 15 to 25 knot range and mid-level\ncapping could limit the spatial extent of the severe weather \npotential.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-60s to lower 70s on\nSaturday night. High temperatures will likely range from the\nmid-80s to lower 90s on Sunday. Heat indices look to be mainly \nin the 90s. There maybe some around 100 in northeast Iowa.\n\nSunday night and Monday\n\nThe models continue to show that another mesoscale convective\ncomplex will possibly move through the Upper Mississippi River\nValley on Sunday night through Monday. Like its predecessor,\nthere is much uncertainty on its track, and the shear looks\nweak.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-60s to lower 70s on\nSunday night. High temperatures will likely range from the \nmid-80s to lower 90s. Heat indices look to range from the\nmid-80s to mid-90s.",
